**Theorem 6.1 (first possible total state budget).** Under Assumption
3.1 and the fixed-code contract of Definition 3.2, nine is the first
total installed-state budget at which a lawful prescribed binary code
on this entire $\mathcal U_*$ can admit an arbitrary-literal two-call
reader while admitting no same-code representative-root-only two-call
reader. For the particular code (NRE.12), the exact minimum installed
state count among arbitrary-literal readers using at most two calls is
nine. The root-only two-call problem for this code is impossible at
every finite installed-state budget.

**Proof.** Directly reuse *Native cut-birth provenance and literal
past-row recovery*, Theorem 15.4, “root normalization with the original
arbitrary code.” Under that volume's Definition 15.1, every successful
at-most-eight-state, at-most-two-call controller on this same entire
actual fiber, with any prescribed lawful binary computation on the
acquired current data, has a representative-root controller on the
same installed states. The theorem retains its initial map, original
pointwise code, fixed whole halt outputs, state paths and actual call
counts on every original source. It allows internally structure-dependent
codes and all finite native literals, with the acquisition, actual export,
complete cut, recoverable lawful entry and stationary service assumptions
retained here. Smaller successful budgets are included in its at-most-eight
quantifier. No encoder or initialization normalization is substituted for
that fixed-code theorem.

It follows that no budget at most eight has the asserted existence
separation. Theorem 4.1 supplies such a separation's upper side at nine,
and Theorem 5.1 supplies its same-code root-only impossibility. This
proves the first possible budget. If an arbitrary-literal reader for
(NRE.12) used at most eight states and two calls, the directly cited
Theorem 15.4 would give a same-code root reader with two calls,
contradicting Theorem 5.1. Hence its arbitrary-literal minimum is at
least nine; Theorem 4.1 attains nine. The final assertion is exactly
Theorem 5.1. This uses the existing eight-state theorem as a supplier,
without giving it a new proof or counting its normalization as new
mathematical content. $\square$

